To ensure the reflector doesn't overdo the fill light, it's essential to position it correctly and adjust its distance and orientation. A good starting point is to place the reflector at a 45-degree angle to your face, about 2-3 feet away. This will help bounce a subtle amount of light onto the side of your face, filling in shadows without overpowering the main light source.

As for the size of the reflector, a smaller to medium-sized one (around 12-24 inches in diameter) should be sufficient for a headshot. A larger reflector might be too overpowering, while a smaller one might not provide enough fill light.

To fine-tune the reflector's effect, you can experiment with different distances and angles. If you notice the fill light is too strong, try moving the reflector farther away or adjusting its angle to reduce the amount of light being bounced onto your face. Conversely, if the fill light is too subtle, you can move the reflector closer or adjust its angle to increase the amount of light being reflected.

It's also worth noting that the type of reflector used can affect the quality of the fill light. A soft, white reflector or a dedicated photography reflector with a soft, diffused surface can help create a more natural, subtle fill light. Avoid using a reflector with a shiny or metallic surface, as it can create harsh, specular highlights.

